{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<pre><code>im.WithPackages([]library.Package{
    {
        Name: "htmx", Version: "2.0.4",
        Require: library.Includes{{"{{"}}File: "htmx.esm.min.js", As: "htmx"}},
    },
    {
        Name: "bootstrap", Version: "5.3.3",
        Require: library.Includes{{"{{"}}File: "css/bootstrap.min.css", As: "bootstrap"}},
    },
})</code></pre>
<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Name</code></td><td>The provider’s package identifier</td></tr><tr><td><code>Version</code></td><td>An explicit version; empty delegates resolution to the provider</td></tr><tr><td><code>Require</code></td><td>File paths to include in the public asset tree</td></tr><tr><td><code>Include.As</code></td><td>The import name or stylesheet key</td></tr><tr><td><code>Package.Provider</code></td><td>An optional provider override for this package</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>File paths and module names</h2>
<p>Required paths match the provider’s file listing. Use exact package-relative paths when getting started. The alias <code>htmx</code> lets browser code write <code>import htmx from "htmx"</code> independently of the original filename.</p>
<p>A JavaScript file is not automatically an ES module. Choose an ESM distribution for <code>type="module"</code> imports. Classic global scripts and CommonJS distributions need a compatible loading strategy.</p>
<h2>Append or replace the package list</h2>
<pre><code>im.WithPackage(library.Package{
    Name: "bootstrap", Version: "5.3.3",
    Require: library.Includes{{"{{"}}File: "css/bootstrap.min.css", As: "bootstrap"}},
})</code></pre>
<p><code>WithPackages</code> replaces the configured list. <code>WithPackage</code> appends one entry. Prepare the final set before generating output; changing dependencies after serving starts requires a new preparation cycle.</p>
<h2>What the package does not build</h2>
<p>It downloads and publishes files. It does not transpile TypeScript, bundle JavaScript, compile Tailwind, or rewrite every import in an arbitrary dependency graph. Choose provider outputs whose module imports remain resolvable in your browser setup.</p>
</article>
